lly.\n","   - Cases where regex found \"Only Mentioning\" but structured found \"Violation\": 150\n","   - Cases where regex found \"Only Mentioning\" but structured found \"No Violation\": 48\n","   - Total \"Only Mentioning\" by regex: 253\n","   - Total \"Only Mentioning\" by structured: 56\n","3. Both methods largely agree when an explicit \"Violation\" or \"No Violation\" phrase is present and directly maps to the structured outcome, but discrepancies highlight the limitations of regex on less uniformly phrased text.\n"]}],"source":["print('\\nComparison of regex-based finding with structured finding:')\n","comparison_df = pd.crosstab(df_art8['finding'], df_art8['finding_structured'])\n","display(comparison_df)\n","\n","print('\\nSummary of differences and similarities:')\n","print('1. Structured extraction is generally more precise and identifies more cases with a clear outcome (Violation/No Violation) than the regex approach.')\n","print(f'   - Total structured Violations: {comparison_df[\"Violation\"].sum()}')\n","print(f'   - Total regex Violations: {comparison_df.loc[\"Violation\"].sum()}')\n","print(f'   - Total structured No Violations: {comparison_df[\"No Violation\"].sum()}')\n","print(f'   - Total regex No Violations: {0} (None found by regex)')\n","print('2. The regex method tends to categorize more cases as \"Only Mentioning\" because it relies on explicit phrasing that might not always be present in the plain text, even when a clear outcome exists structurally.')\n","print(f'   - Cases where regex found \"Only Mentioning\" but structured found \"Violation\": {comparison_df.loc[\"Only Mentioning\", \"Violation\"]}')\n","print(f'   - Cases where regex found \"Only Mentioning\" but structured found \"No Violation\": {comparison_df.loc[\"Only Mentioning\", \"No Violation\"]}')\n","print(f'   - Total \"Only Mentioning\" by regex: {comparison_df.loc[\"Only Mentioning\"].sum()}')\n","print(f'   - Total \"Only Mentioning\" by structured: {comparison_df[\"Only Mentioning\"].sum()}')\n","print('3. Both methods largely agree when an explicit \"Violation\" or \"No Violation\" phrase is present and directly maps to the structured outcome, but discrepancies highlight the limitations of regex on less uniformly phrased text.')"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"ek8WxtV4wJE3"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","Why Structured conclusion is superior to Regex:\n","\n","1. The structured `conclusion` provides a more comprehensive and accurate representation of the data compared to using Regex pattern matching on the `__conclusion` text.\n","\n","\n","2. Higher Accuracy for Violations: In a comparison of 2010–2023 Article 8 cases, the structured approach identified 983 violations, whereas the Regex approach only found 909.\n","\n","\n","3. Captures Complex Findings: The structured column correctly identifies cases with combined article findings (e.g., \"Violation of Article 14+8\") that standard Regex patterns often miss.\n","\n","\n","4. Resilience to Phrasing: It uses the ECHR's internal labeling (structured as a list of dictionaries with fields like type and element) rather than relying on human-written plain text, which can vary significantly in phrasing.\n","\n","\n","5. Reduces \"Noise\": Extracting from the structured list identified far fewer \"Only Mentioning\" cases (56) compared to the Regex method (122), leading to a cleaner dataset for modeling."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"_s9Lxh50iVRk"},"source":["\n","\n","---\n","\n","\n","#**2. Parsing**"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"wJUvZOVrpFw5"},"source":["## 2.1 Full Text Structure\n","\n","**Note :**\n","\n","The `full_text` column is a nested dictionary with the following structure:\n","- Level 1: document key (e.g. '001-178343.docx')\n","- Level 2: list of sections, each with `content`, `elements`, `section_name`\n","- Level 3: elements containing paragraph text\n","- Level 4: sub-elements (present in some cases with deeper nesting)\n","\n","Known section names: 'procedure', 'facts', 'law', 'relevant_law',\n","'conclusion', 'introduction', 'appendix', 'toc', 'opinion'."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":41,"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790013608,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"},"user_tz":-480},"id":"pU7bzADBiZaK","outputId":"7f4a410a-abba-4069-ffec-b70f23f2f317"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["<class 'dict'>\n","\n","Top level key: 001-178343.docx\n","Type of value: <class 'list'>\n","\n","Number of sections: 4\n","First section keys: dict_keys(['content', 'elements', 'section_name'])\n","\n","First section content:\n","PROCEDURE 1.  The case originated in three applications (nos. 29431/05, 7070/06 and 5402/07) against the Russian Federation lodged with the Court under Article 34 of the Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms (“the Convention”) by three Russian nationals. Their names and dates of birth, as well as the dates on which they lodged their applications, are listed in the appendix. 2.  The Russian Government (“the Government”) were represented by Mr G. Matyushkin, Representative of the Russian Federation at the European Court of Human Rights, and then by his successor in that office, Mr M. Galperin. Two of the applicants were represented by lawyers whose names are listed in the appendix. 3.  The applicants alleged, in particular, that they had been subjected to covert surveillance in breach of Article 8 of the Convention. One of the applicants also complained of the excessive length of the criminal proceedings. Another complained of the inhuman conditions of de\n","\n","--- Requested Sections ---\n","\n","\n","### Section: PROCEDURE ###\n","PROCEDURE 1.  The case originated in three applications (nos. 29431/05, 7070/06 and 5402/07) against the Russian Federation lodged with the Court under Article 34 of the Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms (“the Convention”) by three Russian nationals. Their names and dates of birth, as well as the dates on which they lodged their applications, are listed in the appendix. 2.  The Russian Government (“the Government”) were represented by Mr G. Matyushkin, Repres...\n","\n","### Section: FACTS ###\n","THE FACTS I.  THE CIRCUMSTANCES OF THE CASE A.  Application no. 29431/05 Zubkov v. Russia 5.  On 12 April 2002 the local police sent to the local investigations committee audio recordings of telephone conversations between the applicant and several persons, and video recordings of their meetings in a flat in Novgorod. The accompanying letter, which the Government presented to the Court, stated that the audio and video recordings had been obtained in the course of covert “operational-search” meas...\n","\n","### Section: LAW ###\n","THE LAW I.  JOINDER OF THE APPLICATIONS 77.  In accordance with Rule 42 § 1 of the Rules of Court, the Court decides to join the applications, given their factual and legal similarities. II.  ALLEGED VIOLATION OF ARTICLE 8 OF THE CONVENTION 78.  The applicants complained that the interception of their telephone communications and, for one of the applicants, the covert filming of his meetings with acquaintances in a rented flat, had violated their right to respect for their private life, correspo...\n","\n","### Section: CONCLUSION ###\n","FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT, UNANIMOUSLY, 1.  Decides to join the applications; 2.  Decides, having regard to the terms of the Government’s declaration, and the arrangements for ensuring compliance with the undertakings referred to therein, to strike Mr Gorbunov’s application out of its list of cases in accordance with Article 37 § 1 (c) of the Convention in so far as it concerns the complaints under Articles 3 of the Convention of the allegedly inhuman conditions of detention and transport and...\n"]}],"source":["# Helper function to recursively extract all content\n","def extract_content_recursive(data):\n","    text_parts = []\n","    if isinstance(data, dict):\n","        if 'content' in data and isinstance(data['content'], str):\n","            text_parts.append(data['content'])\n","        if 'elements' in data and isinstance(data['elements'], list):\n","            for element in data['elements']:\n","                text_parts.extend(extract_content_recursive(element))\n","    return text_parts\n","\n","first_val = df_art8['full_text'].iloc[0]\n","print(type(first_val))\n","print()\n","\n","# If it's a dict, look at the first key\n","if isinstance(first_val, dict):\n","    first_key = list(first_val.keys())[0]\n","    print(f'Top level key: {first_key}')\n","    first_section = first_val[first_key]\n","    print(f'Type of value: {type(first_section)}')\n","    print()\n","    # Look at the first section\n","    if isinstance(first_section, list):\n","        print(f'Number of sections: {len(first_section)}')\n","        print(f'First section keys: {first_section[0].keys() if isinstance(first_section[0], dict) else first_section[0]}')\n","        print()\n","        print('First section content:')\n","        # Print the first section's content, up to 1000 characters, using the recursive extractor\n","        all_content_first_section = extract_content_recursive(first_section[0])\n","        print(' '.join(all_content_first_section)[:1000])\n","\n","        # Print specific sections as requested, using the recursive extractor\n","        requested_sections = ['procedure', 'law', 'facts', 'conclusion']\n","        print('\\n--- Requested Sections ---\\n')\n","        for section in first_section:\n","            if isinstance(section, dict) and 'section_name' in section and section['section_name'] in requested_sections:\n","                print(f\"\\n### Section: {section['section_name'].upper()} ###\")\n","                full_section_text = extract_content_recursive(section)\n","       