     4\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"6+8+35+41\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 1,\n        \"min\": 0,\n        \"max\": 4,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 5,\n        \"samples\": [\n          4,\n          2,\n          1\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}"}},"metadata":{}}],"source":["\n","# 3. Initialize a nested dictionary, yearly_top_article_set_counts\n","yearly_top_article_set_counts = {label: {year: 0 for year in range(2010, 2024)} for label in article_set_labels.values()}\n","\n","# 4. Iterate through each row of the df_mixed_articles DataFrame.\n","# 5. For each case, extract its year and article_numbers list.\n","# 6. Clean the article_numbers by converting each item to a string, stripping whitespace, and converting numeric strings to integers.\n","# 7. If cleaned_articles is not empty, create a unique, sorted tuple of these article numbers (unique_sorted_articles_tuple).\n","# 8. Check if this unique_sorted_articles_tuple is present in your top_10_article_set_tuples.\n","# 9. If it is one of the top 10 sets and the year exists as a key in the corresponding entry in yearly_top_article_set_counts, increment its count by 1.\n","for index, row in df_mixed_articles.iterrows():\n","    year = row['year']\n","    articles_list = row['article_numbers']\n","\n","    if isinstance(articles_list, list) and articles_list:\n","        cleaned_articles = []\n","        for item in articles_list:\n","            s_item = str(item).strip()\n","            if s_item.isdigit():\n","                cleaned_articles.append(int(s_item))\n","\n","        if cleaned_articles:\n","            unique_sorted_articles_tuple = tuple(sorted(list(set(cleaned_articles))))\n","\n","            # Check if this article set is one of the top 10\n","            if unique_sorted_articles_tuple in top_10_article_set_tuples:\n","                label = article_set_labels[unique_sorted_articles_tuple]\n","                if year in yearly_top_article_set_counts[label]:\n","                    yearly_top_article_set_counts[label][year] += 1\n","\n","# Convert the dictionary to a DataFrame for easier visualization and further processing\n","df_yearly_top_article_sets = pd.DataFrame(yearly_top_article_set_counts).sort_index()\n","\n","print(\"Yearly counts for the top 10 most frequent article sets:\")\n","display(df_yearly_top_article_sets)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"nyGdtJbyh2Ni"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The 8+41 and 8+35+41 combinations are the most consistent combinations consistently across virtually every year, confirming that these are structural companions to Article 8  litigation rather reflecting any particular than topical trend.\n","\n","\n","The 8+13 combination shows a marked increase from 2021, peaking at 17 cases in 2022, suggesting a period where effective remedy complaints alongside Article 8 were particularly active. Similarly, 3+8+13 spikes sharply in 2022 (13 cases) and 2023 (10 cases), while 3+5+8+13, this complex four-article combination likely reflects a specific pattern of detention-related cases, possibly connected to the surge in Russian cases identified earlier.\n","\n","\n","The 2022 spike is visible across most combinations, consistent with the overall volume increase attributable to the processing of pending Russian cases following Russia's expulsion from the Council of Europe. This suggests that the 2022 surge was not confined to simple Article 8 cases but extended across multiple article combinations, reflecting the broad range of human rights complaints in the Russian caseload."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"81a48fbf","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":409},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790643572,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":577,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"d032c5f9-5111-487d-f63d-f1ad21447f01"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1600x900 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# 4. Visualize the trends\n","plt.figure(figsize=(16, 9))\n","sns.lineplot(data=df_yearly_top_article_sets, marker='o', dashes=False)\n","\n","plt.title('Yearly Trends of Top 10 Article Sets in Mixed Article Cases (2010-2023)', fontsize=16)\n","plt.xlabel('Year', fontsize=12)\n","plt.ylabel('Number of Cases', fontsize=12)\n","plt.xticks(range(2010, 2024), rotation=45, ha='right')\n","plt.grid(axis='y', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7)\n","plt.legend(title='Article Set', bbox_to_anchor=(1.05, 1), loc='upper left')\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"5bbe2000"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","**Key Trends Observed in Yearly Frequencies of Top Article Sets:**\n","\n","1.  **Overall Increase followed by Decline**: Most article sets show a general increase in frequency towards the later years, particularly peaking around 2021-2022, consistent with the overall spike in case volume attributable to the processing of pending Russian cases. A sharp decline follows in 2023 as that caseload was exhausted.\n","\n","2.  **Prominence of Procedural and Remedial Articles**: Combinations including Article 41 (just satisfaction) and Article 35 (admissibility), such as 8+41, 8+35+41, and 6+8+35+41, appear consistently across all years, confirming that financial remedies and admissibility are recurring structural features of mixed Article 8 cases rather than topical trends.\n","\n","3.  **Notable Rise in Article 13 Combinations**: The combination 8+13 shows a marked increase, particularly in 2021-2022, suggesting a growing number of cases involving Article 8 disputes that also address the inadequacy of domestic of domestic remedies. The 3+8+13 combination follows a similar pattern, remaining elevated into 2023.\n","\n","4.  **Impact of Geopolitical Events**: The year 2023 shows a sharp decline for most article sets, mirroring the overall decrease in case volume observed after Russia's expulsion from the Council of Europe in March 2022, which significantly impacted the number of cases processed by the Court.\n","\n","\n","5.  **Diverse Legal Contexts**: While some general trends are visible, the varying patterns among different article sets highlight the diverse legal contexts in which Article 8 is invoked, often paired with other substantive rights or procedural articles.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"53Y42_FA1LXv"},"source":["###4.4 Importance\n","\n","**Note :**\n","\n","**Case Reports (Level 1)**: These are \"Landmark\" judgments. They represent the most important cases decided by the Court each year. They are usually decided by the Grand Chamber and establish new legal principles or change existing ones.\n","\n","**High Importance (Level 2)**: These are \"Key\" cases. They make a significant contribution to the Court’s case law by providing important developments, clarifications, or applications of the Convention.\n","\n","**Medium Importance (Level 3)**: These are cases of lower legal interest. They usually apply well-settled case law to a specific set of facts without creating new precedents.\n","\n","**Low Importance (Level 4)**: These are \"Repetitive\" cases. Most Committee judgments fall here. They simply reiterate established law to resolve a high volume of similar applications."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"F1nTBFZxMcOl"},"source":["####4.4.1 Importance vs Violation Status"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"D6qsjktQP-4v","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":716},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790643665,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":74,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"1d543b34-4e36-4c09-c013-25bb1903413e"},"output