o account all the packages that are installed. This behaviour is the source of the following dependency conflicts.\n","access 1.1.10.post3 requires scipy>=1.14.1, but you have scipy 1.13.1 which is incompatible.\n","tsfresh 0.21.1 requires scipy>=1.14.0; python_version >= \"3.10\", but you have scipy 1.13.1 which is incompatible.\u001b[0m\u001b[31m\n","\u001b[0mSuccessfully installed flashtext-2.7 gensim-4.4.0 scattertext-0.2.2 scipy-1.13.1\n","Scattertext library installed and data prepared for visualization.\n"]}],"source":["import sys\n","!{sys.executable} -m pip install scattertext\n","\n","import scattertext as st\n","\n","# Ensure the 'outcome_category' column is of string type for Scattertext\n","df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] = df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'].astype(str)\n","\n","print(\"Scattertext library installed and data prepared for visualization.\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"MCKiXGF533TS"},"source":["####5.2.1 Overview (Full Case)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"069cd800","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790756917,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":51564,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"013b8071-0b8a-4fd1-d535-936ffc2f6e02"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Scattertext corpus built.\n","Interactive Scattertext visualization saved to 'article8_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\n","Open the HTML file through your own browser\n"]}],"source":["import scattertext as st\n","from IPython.display import HTML\n","\n","# 1. Create a Scattertext Corpus\n","# Ensure that the 'parsed_full_text' column contains string data\n","df_filtered_cases['parsed_full_text'] = df_filtered_cases['parsed_full_text'].astype(str)\n","\n","corpus = st.CorpusFromPandas(\n","    df_filtered_cases,\n","    category_col='outcome_category',\n","    text_col='parsed_full_text',\n","    nlp=st.whitespace_nlp_with_sentences\n",").build()\n","print(\"Scattertext corpus built.\")\n","\n","# 2. Generate the interactive HTML visualization with reduced term frequency\n","html = st.produce_scattertext_explorer(\n","    corpus,\n","    category='Violation',\n","    category_name='Violation',\n","    not_category_name='No Violation',\n","    width_in_pixels=1000,\n","    metadata=df_filtered_cases['title'],\n","    minimum_term_frequency=50 # Added parameter to reduce the number of terms\n",")\n","\n","# 3. Save the HTML to a file\n","with open('article8_violation_vs_no_violation.html', 'w') as f:\n","    f.write(html)\n","\n","print(\"Interactive Scattertext visualization saved to 'article8_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\")\n","print (\"Open the HTML file through your own browser\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"8VGV0XuEx9O5"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The scatter plot reveals meaningful differences in the vocabulary associated with each outcome class. Words appearing in the upper-left quadrant are frequent in violation cases but rare in non-violation cases, while words in the lower-right are frequent in non-violation cases but rare in violations.\n","On the violation side, terms including **\"cia\", \"military\", \"detainees\", \"rendition\", \"aircraft\", \"interrogation\", and \"afghanistan\"** refer to extraordinary rendition and secret detention cases, a category of serious Article 8 violations involving the transfer of individuals to third countries for interrogation outside legal oversight. Additional terms such as **\"forces\", \"marginal\", and \"shelling\"** further suggest that cases involving state military or security operations feature disproportionately among violations, demonstrating that such conduct is difficult to justify as proportionate interference with private life under Article 8(2).\n","\n","\n","On the non-violation side, notable terms include **\"vetting\" and \"RIPA\"** (Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000), relating to security vetting, employment screening, and surveillance cases — areas where the Court has consistently granted states a wide margin of appreciation, particularly in national security contexts, resulting in non-violation findings.\n","The Characteristic column lists words that appear frequently across both classes, such as **\"applicant\", \"court\", \"proceedings\", \"convention\", and \"interference\"**. These are terms common to almost every judgment regardless of outcome and reflect the shared legal vocabulary of the ECHR corpus rather than being predictive of either class."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"IqRvTJXu39N9"},"source":["####5.2.2 Facts Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"xL-CKE0T4E0I","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790786799,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":29880,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"c48151e3-c043-4b01-8dff-86693457c2c7"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Scattertext corpus built for 'facts' section.\n","Interactive Scattertext visualization for 'facts' section saved to 'article8_facts_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\n","Open the HTML file through your own browser\n"]}],"source":["# NOTE: Helper functions extract_content_recursive, get_facts_text, and creation of df_facts_filtered\n","# have been moved to an earlier cell (8ec5cc3f) to ensure df_facts_filtered is defined prior to this cell.\n","\n","# 1. Create a Scattertext Corpus for the 'facts' section\n","corpus_facts = st.CorpusFromPandas(\n","    df_facts_filtered,\n","    category_col='outcome_category',\n","    text_col='parsed_facts_text',\n","    nlp=st.whitespace_nlp_with_sentences\n",").build()\n","print(\"Scattertext corpus built for 'facts' section.\")\n","\n","# 2. Generate the interactive HTML visualization for 'facts' section\n","html_facts = st.produce_scattertext_explorer(\n","    corpus_facts,\n","    category='Violation',\n","    category_name='Violation (Facts)',\n","    not_category_name='No Violation (Facts)',\n","    width_in_pixels=1000,\n","    metadata=df_facts_filtered['title'],\n","    minimum_term_frequency=30  # Added parameter to reduce the number of terms\n","\n",")\n","# Lower threshold than full text since facts/law sections have smaller per-case vocabulary\n","\n","# 3. Save the HTML to a file\n","with open('article8_facts_violation_vs_no_violation.html', 'w') as f:\n","    f.write(html_facts)\n","\n","print(\"Interactive Scattertext visualization for 'facts' section saved to 'article8_facts_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\")\n","print (\"Open the HTML file through your own browser\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"v35H93PL2MZt"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The Facts section scatter plot reinforces and refines the patterns observed in the full case analysis. On the violation side, terms such as **\"cia\", \"secret\", \"detainees\", \"military\", \"prisoners\", \"russian\", and \"aircraft\"** cluster together, pointing to cases involving state military and security operations as dominant factors in Article 8 violations. The term \"secret\" here refers to detention facilities and surveillance measures rather than confidentiality in a general sense.\n","\n","On the non-violation side, the vocabulary shifts toward family and social life contexts, with terms such as **\"foster\", \"relationship\", \"education\", \"girls\", \"religious\", and \"expulsion\"** suggesting that cases concerning child welfare, custody arrangements, and religious upbringing feature prominently among non-violations. Notably, \"secret\" appears here too but in an entirely different context, likely referring to closed or confidential child proceedings, which the Court has consistently treated as a legitimate and proportionate procedural measure. This contrast illustrates how the same word can carry fundamentally different legal significance depending on outcome class, highlighting a limitation of purely lexical approaches to classification.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"XW5nZzD14AMk"},"source":["####5.2.3 Law Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"91515191","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790799407,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":12603,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"76520335-3b02-4f9a-a727-b0dc462ece6d"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Scattertext corpus built for 'law' section.\n","Interactive Scattertext visualization for 'law' section saved to 'article8_law_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\n","Open the HTML file through your own browser\n"]}],"source":["# NOTE: Helper functions extract_content_recursive, get_law_text, and creation of df_law_filtered\n","# have been moved to an earlier cell (Fch9-tfzCLvI) to ensure df_law_filtered is defined prior to this cell.\n","\n","# 1. Create a Scattertext Corpus for the 'law' section\n","corpus_law = st.CorpusFromPandas(\n","    df_law_filtered,\n","    category_col='outcome_category',\n","    text_col='parsed_law_text',\n","    nlp=st.whitespace_nlp_with_sentences\n",").build()\n","print(\"Scattertext corpus built for 'law' section.\")\n","\n","# 2. Generate the interactive HTML visualization for 'law' section\n","html_law = st.produce_scattertext_explorer(\n","    corpus_law,\n","    category='Violation',\n","    category_name='Violation (Law)',\n","    not_category_name='No Violation (Law)',\n","    width_in_pixels=1000,\n","    metadata=df_law_filtered['title'],\n","    minimum_term_frequency=30,  # Added parameter to reduce the number of terms\n",")\n","# Lower threshold than full text since facts/law sections have smaller per-case vocabulary\n","\n","# 3. Save the HTML to a file\n","with open('article8_law_violation_vs_no_violation.html', 'w') as f:\n","    f.write(html_law)\n","\n","print(\"Interactive Scattertext visualization for 'law' section saved to 'article8_law_violation_vs_no_violation.html'.\")\n","print (\"Open the HTML file through your own browser\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"GUF1p9KY5QFI"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The Law section scatter plot on the violation side reveals prominent terms such as **\"incurred\", \"expenses\", \"pecuniary\", \"costs\", \"award\", and \"injured\"**. These terms do not reflect the Court's reasoning on whether a violation occurred, but rather the just satisfaction assessment under Article 41 that follows once a violation has already been found. This is consistent with the high frequency of pairing between Article 8 and Article 41 observed in the dataset. The presence of these terms confirms the data leakage concern raised by Medvedeva et al. (2018) and O'Sullivan and Beel (2019), both of whom excluded the Law section on the grounds that it contains outcome-consequent language that may allow a classifier to infer the verdict from its consequences rather than from substantive reasoning, a limitation that will need to be carefully considered during model training.\n","\n","On the non-violation side, terms such as **\"vetting\", \"religious\", \"birth\", \"expulsion\", \"women\", \"dwelling\", \"relationship\", and \"church\"** reflect the substantive areas in which the Court most frequently declines to find a violation. These correspond to domains where the Court has historically granted states a wide margin of appreciation, i.e. religious practice, family formation, housing, and immigration. This suggests that despite the leakage risk, the Law section can also be informative."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"09495435"},"source":["##5.3 Concordance Analysis\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"979b7ccc","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790799458,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":40,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"7424060e-0039-4967-caeb-c009819c5198"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Key terms defined and get_concordance function created.\n"]}],"source":["# 1. Define a list of key_terms to analyze\n","key_terms = ['family life', 'private life', 'best interests', 'pecuniary damage', 'margin of appreciation']\n","\n","# 2. Create a function, get_concordance\n","def get_concordance(text, search_term, window_size=50):\n","    \"\"\"Finds all occurrences of a search_term in text and returns concordances.\"\"\"\n","    concordances = []\n","    # Use re.IGNORECASE for case-insensitive search\n","    for match in re.finditer(re.escape(search_term), text, re.IGNORECASE):\n","        start_idx = max(0, match.start() - window_size)\n","        end_idx = min(len(text), match.end() + window_size)\n","\n","        # Extract the context around the search term\n","        context = text[start_idx:end_idx]\n","\n","        # Highlight the search term (optional, but good for display)\n","        # Using a placeholder for now, actual highlighting can be done at display time\n","        highlighted_context = context[:match.start()-start_idx] + \\\n","                              '**' + text[match.start():match.end()] + '**' + \\\n","                              context[match.end()-start_idx:]\n","        concordances.append(highlighted_context)\n","    return concordances\n","\n","print(\"Key terms defined and get_concordance function created.\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"aXpZbrpQ91mi"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The key terms selected for concordance analysis were chosen based on their prominence in the n-gram and scatter plot analyses, and their importance in the interpretation of Article 8 judgments. **\"Family life\"** and **\"private life\"** were selected as the core protected interests under Article 8, featuring consistently across both the n-gram and scatter plot analyses. **\"Best interests\"** emerged in the n-gram analysis and is associated with child-related cases, where the Court's assessment of whether domestic authorities adequately weighed the child's interests is frequently determinative of the outcome. **\"Margin of appreciation\"** was included as it appeared in the Law section analysis and represents the principal mechanism through which the Court defers to state discretion, making it one of the most outcome-relevant phrases in Article 8 reasoning. Finally, **\"pecuniary damage\"** was retained as it directly illustrates the presence of outcome-consequent language in the Law section, providing concrete textual evidence for the data leakage concern identified earlier.\n","\n","\n","Terms that appeared frequently but uniformly across both classes, such as **\"district court\"**, **\"domestic courts\"**, and **\"criminal proceedings\"**, were excluded on the basis that they reflect procedural structure rather than substantive reasoning and are unlikely to carry discriminative value for classification."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"a756af75","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":224},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790799963,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":501,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"f9878c8d-110e-48ec-ab25-9a1cc4263a55"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Generated 2,640 concordances for 5 key terms across 154 cases.\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["       itemid outcome_category     key_term  \\\n","0  001-106968        Violation  family life   \n","1  001-106968        Violation  family life   \n","2  001-106968        Violation  family life   \n","3  001-106968        Violation  family life   \n","4  001-106968        Violation  family life   \n","\n","                                         concordance  \n","0  .  The applicant alleged that he and his child