lica\n","\n","\n","--- Sample Concordances for 'margin of appreciation' (Violation Cases) ---\n","Sample 1: ing their living conditions, secondly, that the States are accorded a wide **margin of appreciation** in housing matters, and thirdly, that the applicants have not convincingly\n","\n","Sample 2: he Court takes the view that the Contracting States must be granted a wide **margin of appreciation** in assessing the need to establish a legal framework governing the conditi\n","\n","Sample 3: ing on the application of secret intelligence devices, an excessively wide **margin of appreciation** may not be granted for the authorities (e.g. Valenzuela Contreras v. Spain\n","\n","Sample 4: C], §§ 101-02, and Strand Lobben and Others, § 210, both cited above). The **margin of appreciation** to be granted to the competent national authorities will vary in accordanc\n","\n","Sample 5: On the contrary, in Article 8 cases the Court has generally understood the **margin of appreciation** to mean that, where the independent and impartial domestic courts have car\n","\n","\n","--- Sample Concordances for 'margin of appreciation' (No Violation Cases) ---\n","Sample 1: own case-law and the Government's submissions, notwithstanding the State's **margin of appreciation** as well as the fact that A and B were eventually surrendered to the applic\n","\n","Sample 2: elves is, in principle, a matter that falls within the Contracting States’ **margin of appreciation**. In this connection, there are different ways of ensuring “respect for pri\n","\n","Sample 3: tion of the Parliamentary Assembly – paragraph 51 above). Accordingly, the **margin of appreciation** should in principle be the same in both cases. 88.  Where the balancing ex\n","\n","Sample 4: ticising the regime then in force (see §§ 42-43). 88.  There is a narrowed **margin of appreciation** available to States when setting retention limits for the biometric data o\n","\n","Sample 5: nd commented as follows: “In S. and Marper the ECtHR, when considering the **margin of appreciation** in the case of those who were acquitted, placed some reliance upon the fac\n","\n"]}],"source":["# Display sample concordances for each key term and outcome category\n","for term in key_terms:\n","    for outcome in ['Violation', 'No Violation']:\n","        print(f\"\\n--- Sample Concordances for '{term}' ({outcome} Cases) ---\")\n","\n","        filtered = df_concordances[\n","            (df_concordances['key_term'] == term) &\n","            (df_concordances['outcome_category'] == outcome)\n","        ]\n","\n","        if filtered.empty:\n","            print(\"No concordances found.\")\n","            continue\n","\n","        samples = filtered['concordance'].sample(min(5, len(filtered))).tolist()\n","\n","        for i, conc in enumerate(samples):\n","            print(f\"Sample {i+1}: {conc}\\n\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"e1npYFOKE4Mr"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","1. For **\"family life\"**, the violation concordances show the Court actively reasoning about whether an action constituted an interference with the applicant's right to respect for family life, including in contexts involving care orders, custody disputes, and the recognition of family ties outside traditional family structures. In contrast, the non-violation concordances more frequently appear in the context of quoting domestic law or the Convention text itself, or in cases where the Court assessed whether the interference was proportionate to a legitimate aim. This distinction suggests that in violation cases the term appears within the Court's substantive reasoning, whereas in non-violation cases it more often appears in formulaic or prefatory contexts.\n","\n","2. For **\"private life\"**, the violation concordances show the Court extending the scope of protection broadly, describing private life as \"a broad term not susceptible to exhaustive definition\" and finding interference in contexts such as incapacitation proceedings and workplace correspondence. Non-violation concordances, by contrast, show the Court acknowledging an interference but finding it justified, or examining whether domestic courts adequately protected the applicant's private life before concluding they did. Across both terms, the concordance analysis illustrates that the same word carries structurally different legal meaning depending on its position in the Court's reasoning.\n","\n","3. The concordances for **\"best interests\"** reveal a meaningful difference in how the term is deployed across outcome classes. In violation cases, the Court found that domestic authorities failed to give \"appropriate weight\" to the child's best interests, or made bare assertions that an outcome was in the child's best interests \"without explaining why\". In non-violation cases, the term appears in contexts where the Court was satisfied that authorities had genuinely engaged with and balanced the child's interests against competing considerations or where it is invoked as a general principle rather than as a critique of its application in the specific case.\n","\n","4. The concordances for **\"pecuniary damage\"**, which in practice appear almost exclusively as \"non-pecuniary damage\" show phrases such as \"awards the applicant EUR in respect of non-pecuniary damage\" and \"the applicant must have suffered non-pecuniary damage\", indicating that the term is used in the context of awarding just satisfaction after a violation has already been found. This confirms the data leakage concern previously identified, where outcome-consequent language in the Law section directly signals the verdict rather than the reasoning behind it. In non-violation cases, by contrast, the term appears in domestic law provisions or in the Court examining whether a causal link between damage and violation can be established.\n","\n","5. The concordances for **\"margin of appreciation\"** appear across both outcome classes, but in structurally different positions. In violation cases, the Court acknowledged the margin afforded to the state but ultimately concluded that the national authorities had not taken all necessary steps, the margin is invoked but then overridden. In non-violation cases, the Court itself grants the margin and defers to state judgment, finding that any interference was proportionate and justified. This is one of the most analytically significant patterns in the concordance analysis, as the term's predictive value lies not in its presence alone but in whether the Court ultimately accepts or rejects the state's claimed discretion."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"T56eclXKKabs"},"source":["##5.4 Shifterator"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"y89TLI_ajAcH"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The Shifterator proportion shift analysis quantifies the relative frequency of terms between the two outcome classes, highlighting the specific vocabulary that most heavily tilts a judgment toward one outcome or the other."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"Kdu8R71bcpDO","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790807741,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":7744,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"0466c013-4620-4de5-c83f-71c8e0db3acc"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Collecting shifterator\n","  Downloading shifterator-0.3.0-py3-none-any.whl.metadata (2.1 kB)\n","Requirement already satisfied: matplotlib in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (from shifterator) (3.10.0)\n","Requirement already satisfied: numpy in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (from shifterator) (2.0.2)\n","Requirement already satisfied: contourpy>=1.0.1 in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (from matplotlib->shifterator) (1.3.3)\n","Requirement already satisfied: cycler>=0.10 in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (from matplotlib->shifterator) (0.12.1