n","import re # Make sure re is imported\n","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # Import matplotlib for plt.show()\n","\n","# Ensure stopwords are downloaded\n","nltk.download('stopwords', quiet=True)\n","stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n","\n","# Custom legal stopwords to prevent generic terms from dominating the graph\n","legal_stopwords = {'court', 'applicant', 'article', 'case', 'law', 'government', 'rights', 'echr', 'state', 'national'}\n","all_stopwords = stop_words.union(legal_stopwords)\n","\n","def clean_text_for_shifterator(text):\n","    \"\"\"Strips punctuation, converts to lowercase, and removes stopwords.\"\"\"\n","    if not isinstance(text, str):\n","        return []\n","    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\\s]', '', text) # Remove punctuation and numbers\n","    words = text.lower().split()\n","    return [w for w in words if w not in all_stopwords and len(w) > 2]\n","\n","# Rebuild frequency dictionaries with updated function\n","violation_texts = df_filtered_cases[\n","    df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'Violation'\n","]['parsed_full_text'].tolist()\n","\n","no_violation_texts = df_filtered_cases[\n","    df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'No Violation'\n","]['parsed_full_text'].tolist()\n","\n","freq_violation_words = []\n","for text in violation_texts:\n","    freq_violation_words.extend(clean_text_for_shifterator(text))\n","freq_violation = Counter(freq_violation_words)\n","\n","freq_no_violation_words = []\n","for text in no_violation_texts:\n","    freq_no_violation_words.extend(clean_text_for_shifterator(text))\n","freq_no_violation = Counter(freq_no_violation_words)\n","\n","# Add checks for empty frequency dictionaries or very few words\n","if not freq_violation or not freq_no_violation:\n","    print(\"Warning: One or both frequency dictionaries are empty after cleaning. Cannot generate shift graph.\")\n","elif min(len(freq_violation), len(freq_no_violation)) < 30:\n","    print(f\"Warning: Fewer than 30 unique words after cleaning ({len(freq_violation)} in violation, {len(freq_no_violation)} in no violation). Adjusting top_n.\")\n","    top_n_plot = min(len(freq_violation), len(freq_no_violation), 10) # Plot max 10 if not enough for 30\n","else:\n","    top_n_plot = 30\n","\n","if freq_violation and freq_no_violation: # Only attempt to plot if both dicts are non-empty\n","    shift = sh.ProportionShift(\n","        type2freq_1=freq_no_violation,\n","        type2freq_2=freq_violation\n","    )\n","    shift.get_shift_graph(\n","        top_n=top_n_plot, # Use dynamic top_n\n","        title='Word Shift: Violation vs No Violation',\n","        filename='word_shift_full_case.png',\n","        cumulative_inset=False,\n","        text_size_inset=False\n","    )\n","    plt.show() # Display the plot\n","else:\n","    print(\"Shift graph not generated due to empty frequency dictionaries.\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Note:**\n","\n","The word shift graph confirms the vocabulary patterns identified across the preceding analyses. Words contributing disproportionately to violation cases include \"**convention**\", \"**detention**\", \"**russian**\", \"**criminal**\", \"**investigation**\", \"**eur**\", \"**damage**\", \"**russia**\", and \"**cia**\", reinforcing the finding that state security operations and Russian Federation cases dominate the violation class. Notably, \"**violation**\" and \"**damage**\" appearing on the violation side further confirms the data leakage concern identified in the Law section analysis, where outcome-consequent language directly signals the verdict.\n","\n","Words contributing disproportionately to non-violation cases, \"**child**\", \"**children**\", \"**family**\", \"**contact**\", \"**parents**\", \"**care**\", \"**interests**\", \"**father**\", \"**mother**\", \"**relationship**\", and \"**adoption**\", reinforcing the finding that cases concerning parental contact, custody arrangements, and child welfare feature prominently among non-violations. Together, the word shift graph provides a concise visual summary of the two dominant thematic clusters characterising each outcome class across the dataset."],"metadata":{"id":"3oQ0-8uM8QUB"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"nWJvNVjGsv_z"},"source":["###5.4.1 Overview (Full Case)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"mB4NSJmBgD7M","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790811965,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":1445,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"1d80f907-d55b-4192-ec0b-9b39e3125a3f"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Cleaning Full Case text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 700x1500 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# Ensure stopwords are downloaded\n","nltk.download('stopwords', quiet=True)\n","stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n","\n","# Custom legal stopwords to prevent generic terms from dominating the graph\n","legal_stopwords = {'court', 'applicant', 'article', 'case', 'law', 'government', 'rights', 'echr', 'state', 'national'}\n","all_stopwords = stop_words.union(legal_stopwords)\n","\n","def clean_text(text):\n","    \"\"\"Strips punctuation, converts to lowercase, and removes stopwords.\"\"\"\n","    if not isinstance(text, str):\n","        return []\n","    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\\s]', '', text)\n","    words = text.lower().split()\n","    return [w for w in words if w not in all_stopwords and len(w) > 2]\n","\n","# 1. Apply cleaning to the parsed_full_text\n","print(\"Cleaning Full Case text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\")\n","violation_full_words = []\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'Violation']['parsed_full_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: violation_full_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","no_violation_full_words = []\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'No Violation']['parsed_full_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: no_violation_full_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","freq_violation_full = Counter(violation_full_words)\n","freq_no_violation_full = Counter(no_violation_full_words)\n","\n","# 2. Initialize the Proportion Shift\n","proportion_shift_full = sh.ProportionShift(type2freq_1=freq_no_violation_full,\n","                                           type2freq_2=freq_violation_full)\n","\n","# 3. Generate the shift graph\n","# FIX: get_shift_graph returns a single Axes object. Both insets disabled to bypass matplotlib bug.\n","ax = proportion_shift_full.get_shift_graph(system_names=['No Violation', 'Violation'],\n","                                           title='Word Shift: No Violation vs. Violation (Cleaned Full Text)',\n","                                           cumulative_inset=False,\n","                                           text_size_inset=False)\n","plt.show()\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"y9xZsukLhRlE"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","When analysing across all sections, the violation side combines state security vocabulary with outcome-consequent legal terminology, featuring terms such as \"**convention**\", \"**detention\", \"police\", \"damage\", \"russian\"**, \"**surveillance**\", \"**secret\", \"criminal\", \"investigation\", \"prison**\", and **\"military**\". Together these point to cases involving coercive state action, law enforcement, detention, and surveillance operations,followed by legal remedies awarded for the breach. The prominence of the word **\"Russian\"** is consistent with Russia's disproportionate representation in the violation class, as identified in the respondent state analysis,  and should be interpreted as a dataset artefact rather than a substantive doctrinal signal.\n","\n","The non-violation side remains remarkably consistent across all sections, dominated by domestic relations vocabulary, **\"child\", \"children\", \"contact\", \"family\", \"parents\", \"care\", \"interests\", \"father\", \"mother\", \"adoption\", \"foster\"**, and \"**birth\"**. This consistency reinforces the observation that the Court frequently defers to domestic authorities in family and child welfare matters, granting a wider margin of appreciation that typically yields non-violation findings. The word shift graph therefore reveals two dominant and thematically distinct Article 8 profiles in the dataset: violation cases centred on state security and coercive action, and non-violation cases centred on family life and child welfare.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"Fx3IdhW6ssPc"},"source":["###5.4.2 Facts Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"Ht52pxj1e_BF","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790812687,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":715,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"1331df80-1133-4a2f-dfd2-790dc4252193"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Cleaning Facts text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 700x1500 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# Ensure stopwords are downloaded\n","nltk.download('stopwords', quiet=True)\n","stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n","\n","# Custom legal stopwords to prevent generic terms from dominating the graph\n","legal_stopwords = {'court', 'applicant', 'article', 'case', 'law', 'government', 'rights', 'echr', 'state', 'national'}\n","all_stopwords = stop_words.union(legal_stopwords)\n","\n","def clean_text(text):\n","    \"\"\"Strips punctuation, converts to lowercase, and removes stopwords.\"\"\"\n","    if not isinstance(text, str):\n","        return []\n","    # Remove punctuation and numbers\n","    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\\s]', '', text)\n","    # Lowercase and split\n","    words = text.lower().split()\n","    # Remove stopwords and ultra-short tokens\n","    return [w for w in words if w not in all_stopwords and len(w) > 2]\n","\n","# 1. Apply cleaning to the parsed_facts_text\n","print(\"Cleaning Facts text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\")\n","violation_facts_words = []\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'Violation']['parsed_facts_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: violation_facts_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","no_violation_facts_words = []\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'No Violation']['parsed_facts_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: no_violation_facts_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","freq_violation_facts = Counter(violation_facts_words)\n","freq_no_violation_facts = Counter(no_violation_facts_words)\n","\n","# 2. Initialize the Proportion Shift\n","proportion_shift_facts = sh.ProportionShift(type2freq_1=freq_no_violation_facts,\n","                                            type2freq_2=freq_violation_facts)\n","\n","# 3. Generate the shift graph\n","# FIX: get_shift_graph returns a single Axes object, not a tuple. Both insets disabled to bypass matplotlib bug.\n","ax = proportion_shift_facts.get_shift_graph(system_names=['No Violation', 'Violation'],\n","                                            title='Word Shift: No Violation vs. Violation (Cleaned Facts Sections)',\n","                                            cumulative_inset=False,\n","                                            text_size_inset=False)\n","plt.show()\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"qwp2rQTIgA29"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","In the Facts section, the violation side features terminology associated with coercive state power and law enforcement actions, including \"**detention**\", \"**criminal**\", \"**police**\", \"**cia**\", \"**investigation**\", \"**prosecutor**\", \"**secret**\", \"**military**\", \"**officers**\" and \"**prisoners**\". Together, these terms point to cases involving physical and procedural interferences, such as extraordinary rendition, criminal investigations, and detention, where the Court is also likely to apply strict scrutiny and is less likely to defer to state discretion.\n","\n","The non-violation side presents a picture consistent with the full text analysis, dominated by familial and domestic relations vocabulary such as \"**child**\", \"**parents**\", \"**contact**\",  \"**children**\", **\"care\", \"father\", \"mother\", \"foster\", \"adoption\"**, and \"**school**\". Again, this could be a reflection of the Court's tendency to defer to domestic authorities, in cases involving custody battles, contact orders, and child welfare assessments. In these socially complex domestic arenas, the Court seems to grant a wider margin of appreciation, resulting in non-violation findings where domestic state authorities are deemed better positioned to evaluate the immediate circumstances.\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"YM3cU11bso-C"},"source":["###5.4.3 Law Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"S2vzA4U-Y-9o","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790813307,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":614,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"887d98f4-cd0d-41a4-9b0c-c7de6ed1ddc1"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Cleaning text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 700x1500 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# CODE CELL: Text Cleaning and Shifterator (Law Section)\n","import nltk\n","from nltk.corpus import stopwords\n","import shifterator as sh\n","from collections import Counter\n","\n","# Ensure stopwords are downloaded\n","nltk.download('stopwords', quiet=True)\n","stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n","\n","# Custom legal stopwords to prevent generic terms from dominating the graph\n","legal_stopwords = {'court', 'applicant', 'article', 'case', 'law', 'government', 'rights', 'echr', 'state', 'national'}\n","all_stopwords = stop_words.union(legal_stopwords)\n","\n","def clean_text(text):\n","    \"\"\"Strips punctuation, converts to lowercase, and removes stopwords.\"\"\"\n","    if not isinstance(text, str):\n","        return []\n","    # Remove punctuation and numbers\n","    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\\s]', '', text)\n","    # Lowercase and split\n","    words = text.lower().split()\n","    # Remove stopwords and ultra-short tokens\n","    return [w for w in words if w not in all_stopwords and len(w) > 2]\n","\n","# Ensure 'parsed_law_text' column exists in df_filtered_cases\n","# The get_law_text function should be defined in an earlier cell (e.g., Fch9-tfzCLvI)\n","# If Fch9-tfzCLvI was not run, or df_filtered_cases was reset, this step ensures the column exists.\n","if 'parsed_law_text' not in df_filtered_cases.columns:\n","    # Assuming get_law_text is defined globally or in an earlier executed cell\n","    # If get_law_text itself is missing, that would be a separate error.\n","    df_filtered_cases['parsed_law_text'] = df_filtered_cases['full_text'].apply(get_law_text)\n","\n","# 1. Apply cleaning to the parsed_law_text\n","print(\"Cleaning text and building frequencies... (this may take a moment)\")\n","violation_words = []\n","# [cite: 3] Using df_filtered_cases and 'outcome_category' from the group Colab setup\n","\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'Violation']['parsed_law_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: violation_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","no_violation_words = []\n","df_filtered_cases[df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'] == 'No Violation']['parsed_law_text'].apply(\n","    lambda x: no_violation_words.extend(clean_text(x))\n",")\n","\n","freq_violation = Counter(violation_words)\n","freq_no_violation = Counter(no_violation_words)\n","\n","# 2. Initialize the Proportion Shift\n","proportion_shift = sh.ProportionShift(type2freq_1=freq_no_violation,\n","                                      type2freq_2=freq_violation)\n","\n","# 3. Generate the shift graph\n","# FIX: get_shift_graph returns a single Axes object, not a tuple. Both insets disabled to bypass matplotlib bug.\n","ax = proportion_shift.get_shift_graph(system_names=['No Violation', 'Violation'],\n","                                      title='Word Shift: No Violation vs. Violation (Cleaned Law Sections)',\n","                                      cumulative_inset=False,\n","                                      text_size_inset=False)\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"UKP18HRdd7l5"},"source":[" **Note :**\n","\n"," In the Law section, the violation side is driven heavily by terminology associated with seemingly physical interference and remedial legal action, including \"**convention**\", \"**detention**\", \"**violation**\", \"**damage**\", \"**eur**\", \"**costs**\", \"**expenses**\", \"**nonpecuniary**\", and \"**incurred**\". These terms reflect the just satisfaction assessment that follows a violation finding rather than the reasoning that produced it, and their presence in the Law section risks allowing a classifier to infer the outcome from its consequences.\n","\n"," The non-violation side presents a strikingly different picture, dominated by domestic welfare and social balancing vocabulary such as \"**family**\", \"**life**\", \"**child**\", \"**contact**\", \"**children**\", \"**public**\", \"**interests**\", and \"**private**\", reflecting the Court's tendency to defer to domestic authorities, particularly in family court disputes or when weighing an individual's private life against broader public interests.  This is consistent with the concordance analysis finding that the margin of appreciation is invoked by the Court itself, rather than only argued by the government, in non-violation judgments. In these ethically and socially complex areas, the Court seems to grant a wider margin of appreciation, frequently resulting in non-violation findings.\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"skozgl0_kTvp"},"source":["##5.5. Fighting Words"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"9LEPQKwH9ZKV"},"source":["###5.5.1 Facts Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"be79ec4d","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790813368,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":53,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"1e8b0efa-fecd-491c-949d-857caa99bd6c"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Filtered cases for Importance 1 or 2, with 'Violation' or 'No Violation' outcomes, and non-empty facts text: 18 cases.\n","CountVectorizer initialized for importance levels 1 & 2 facts section with ngram_range=(1, 2).\n"]}],"source":["# 1. Create a new DataFrame, df_filtered_importance\n","# Ensure 'importance' is numeric first, then filter\n","df_filtered_cases['importance'] = pd.to_numeric(df_filtered_cases['importance'], errors='coerce')\n","\n","df_filtered_importance = df_filtered_cases[\n","    (df_filtered_cases['outcome_category'].isin(['Violation', 'No Violation'])) &\n","    (df_filtered_cases['importance'].isin([1, 2]))\n","].copy()\n","\n","# Filter out cases where facts text might be empty after extraction\n","df_filtered_importance = df_filtered_importance[df_filtered_importance['parsed_facts_text'].str.strip() != ''].copy()\n","\n","print(f\"Filtered cases for Importance 1 or 2, with 'Violation' or 'No Violation' outcomes, and non-empty facts text: {len(df_filtered_importance)} cases.\")\n","\n","# 2. Initialize a CountVectorizer\n","cv_importance = CountVectorizer(\n","    ngram_range=(1, 2), # Modified to include unigrams and bigrams\n","    stop_words='english',\n","    min_df=5\n",")\n","print(\"CountVectorizer initialized for importance levels 1 & 2 facts section with ngram_range=(1, 2).\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"8GusiO-guPUU"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The fighting words analysis restricted to importance levels 1 and 2 cases reveals deportation, expulsion and extraordinary rendition vocabulary strongly predicts Violation, while assisted reproduction/IVF vocabulary strongly predicts No Violation. This suggests that for legally significant cases, the subject matter domain is a stronger predictor of outcome than general linguistic features, with potential implications for feature engineering in the modelling stage.\n","\n","It should be noted that the sample is small (n=135, of which 95 Violation and 40 No Violation) and the large log-odds ratios reflect small sample variance rather than robust statistical evidence. Results should be interpreted as qualitative indicators only.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"d37cc410","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":594},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790889180,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":1370,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"5ed48c42-a64b-4f7c-d993-682c49d99e1c"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Top Fighting Words for Violation (Importance 1 & 2):\n","['sustained', 'care', 'notice', 'long term', 'labour', 'father', 'visits', 'settlement', 'directorate', 'child']\n","\n","Top Fighting Words for No Violation (Importance 1 & 2):\n","['secret', 'media', 'ill', 'search', 'territory', 'court justice', 'international law', 'allegedly', '2012', 'existence']\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1200x800 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["X_importance = cv_importance.fit_transform(df_filtered_importance['parsed_facts_text'])\n","words_importance = cv_importance.get_feature_names_out()\n","\n","# Create two separate count arrays for 'Violation' and 'No Violation' cases\n","y_importance = (df_filtered_importance['outcome_category'] == 'Violation').to_numpy()\n","\n","counts_v_importance = np.array(X_importance[y_importance].sum(axis=0)).flatten()\n","counts_nv_importance = np.array(X_importance[~y_importance].sum(axis=0)).flatten()\n","\n","# Calculate the simplified log-odds ratio for each word\n","# Add 1 to avoid division by zero\n","log_odds_importance = np.log((counts_v_importance + 1) / (counts_v_importance.sum() + 1)) - \\\n","                      np.log((counts_nv_importance + 1) / (counts_nv_importance.sum() + 1))\n","\n","# Identify top 10 fighting words for each outcome\n","top_v_indices_importance = log_odds_importance.argsort()[-10:]\n","print('Top Fighting Words for Violation (Importance 1 & 2):')\n","print([words_importance[i] for i in top_v_indices_importance])\n","\n","top_nv_indices_importance = log_odds_importance.argsort()[:10]\n","print('\\nTop Fighting Words for No Violation (Importance 1 & 2):')\n","print([words_importance[i] for i in top_nv_indices_importance])\n","\n","# Create DataFrame for plotting\n","all_top_words_indices_importance = np.concatenate((top_nv_indices_importance, top_v_indices_importance))\n","all_top_words_importance = [words_importance[i] for i in all_top_words_indices_importance]\n","all_log_odds_importance = log_odds_importance[all_top_words_indices_importance]\n","\n","plot_df_importance = pd.DataFrame({\n","    'word': all_top_words_importance,\n","    'log_odds': all_log_odds_importance\n","}).sort_values(by='log_odds', ascending=True).reset_index(drop=True)\n","\n","# Plot using plt.barh to avoid seaborn FutureWarning with custom colour lists\n","colors = plot_df_importance['log_odds'].apply(lambda x: '#d62728' if x > 0 else '#2ca02c').tolist()\n","plt.figure(figsize=(12, 8))\n","plt.barh(plot_df_importance['word'], plot_df_importance['log_odds'], color=colors)\n","plt.title('Top Fighting Words for Facts Section (Importance 1 & 2): No Violation vs. Violation', fontsize=16)\n","plt.xlabel('Log-Odds Ratio (More Positive: Violation | More Negative: No Violation)', fontsize=12)\n","plt.ylabel('Word', fontsize=12)\n","plt.axvline(0, color='gray', linestyle='--', linewidth=0.8)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"PBlF9fwGNm2_"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","The fighting words analysis identifies terms with the highest log-odds ratio of appearing in one outcome class versus the other, providing a more discriminative view of vocabulary than raw frequency alone.\n","\n","In the Facts section, the violation side is dominated by terms associated with state security and border control, including **\"investigator\", \"afghanistan\", \"aircraft\", \"abu\", \"border\", \"expulsion\", \"aliens\", \"aliens act\", and \"flight\"**. Together these point to extraordinary rendition, unlawful deportation, and state security operations as the dominant factual context for Article 8 violations.\n","\n","The non-violation side presents a strikingly different picture, dominated by assisted reproduction and bioethics vocabulary, **\"embryos\", \"embryo\", \"vitro\", \"assisted reproduction\", \"fertilisation\", \"child relationship\", and \"reproduction\"**. This reflects the Court's tendency to grant states a wide margin of appreciation in ethically sensitive areas of social policy, resulting in non-violation findings across this category of cases.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"LxeEkcno9t37"},"source":["###5.5.2 Law Section"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"0gE7v-s7ZlU9","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":630},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1775790898545,"user_tz":-480,"elapsed":390,"user":{"displayName":"Rini P","userId":"15518706882043159565"}},"outputId":"f9bc5cea-3f66-48c2-9137-ebd81340223b"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Filtered cases for Importance 1 or 2, with 'Violation' or 'No Violation' outcomes, and non-empty law text: 18 cases.\n","CountVectorizer initialized for importance levels 1 & 2 law section with ngram_range=(1, 2).\n","Top Fighting Words for Violation (Importance 1 & 2, Law Section):\n","['police', 'maintain', 'children', 'mother', 'father', 'sentence', 'communications', 'parents', 'medical', 'article 14']\n","\n","Top Fighting Words for No Violation (Importance 1 & 2, Law Section):\n","['poland', 'expression', 'transfer', 'federal', 'site', 'content', 'value', 'reporting', 'liability', 'examination case']\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1200x800 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# Filter out cases where law text might be empty\n","df_filtered_importance_law = df_filtered_importance[\n","    df_filtered_importance['parsed_law_text'].str.strip() != ''\n","].copy()\n","\n","print(f\"Filtered cases for Importance 1 or 2, with 'Violation' or 'No Violation' outcomes, and non-empty law text: {len(df_filtered_importance_law)} cases.\")\n","\n","# Initialize CountVectorizer (same settings as facts section)\n","cv_importance_law = CountVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2), stop_words='english', min_df=5)\n","print(\"CountVectorizer initialized for importance levels 1 & 2 law section with ngram_range=(1, 2).\")\n","\n","X_importance_law = cv_importance_law.fit_transform(df_filtered_importance_law['parsed_law_text'])\n","words_importance_law = cv_importance_law.get_feature_names_out()\n","\n","# Count arrays for each outcome\n","y_importance_law = (df_filtered_importance_law['outcome_category'] == 'Violation').to_numpy()\n","counts_v_importance_law = np.array(X_importance_law[y_importance_law].sum(axis=0)).flatten()\n","counts_nv_importance_law = np.array(X_importance_law[~y_importance_law].sum(axis=0)).flatten()\n","\n","# Log-odds ratio\n","log_odds_importance_law = (\n","    np.log((counts_v_importance_law + 1) / (counts_v_importance_law.sum() + 1)) -\n","    np.log((counts_nv_importance_law + 1) / (counts_nv_importance_law.sum() + 1))\n",")\n","\n","# Top 10 fighting words for each outcome\n","top_v_indices_importance_law = log_odds_importance_law.argsort()[-10:]\n","print('Top Fighting Words for Violation (Importance 1 & 2, Law Section):')\n","print([words_importance_law[i] for i in top_v_indices_importance_law])\n","\n","top_nv_indices_importance_law = log_odds_importance_law.argsort()[:10]\n","print('\\nTop Fighting Words for No Violation (Importance 1 & 2, Law Section):')\n","print([words_importance_law[i] for i in top_nv_indices_importance_law])\n","\n","# DataFrame for plotting\n","all_top_words_indices_importance_law = np.concatenate((top_nv_indices_importance_law, top_v_indices_importance_law))\n","all_top_words_importance_law = [words_importance_law[i] for i in all_top_words_indices_importance_law]\n","all_log_odds_importance_law = log_odds_importance_law[all_top_words_indices_importance_law]\n","\n","plot_df_importance_law = pd.DataFrame({\n","    'word': all_top_words_importance_law,\n","    'log_odds': all_log_odds_importance_law\n","}).sort_values(by='log_odds', ascending=True).reset_index(drop=True)\n","\n","# Plot\n","colors = plot_df_importance_law['log_odds'].apply(lambda x: '#d62728' if x > 0 else '#2ca02c').tolist()\n","plt.figure(figsize=(12, 8))\n","plt.barh(plot_df_importance_law['word'], plot_df_importance_law['log_odds'], color=colors)\n","plt.title('Top Fighting Words for Law Section (Importance 1 & 2): No Violation vs. Violation', fontsize=16)\n","plt.xlabel('Log-Odds Ratio (More Positive: Violation | More Negative: No Violation)', fontsize=12)\n","plt.ylabel('Word', fontsize=12)\n","plt.axvline(0, color='gray', linestyle='--', linewidth=0.8)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"bW8DZdtxNuDA"},"source":["**Note :**\n","\n","In the Law section, the violation side shows terms such as **\"aircraft\", \"polish\", \"servicemen\", \"signals\", \"detainees\", \"russian federation\", \"military\", and \"sterilisation\"**, reinforcing the security and military theme observed in the Facts section while introducing **\"sterilisation\" and \"disability\"** as additional violation-associated terms.\n","\n","The non-violation side shifts somewhat, with terms such as **\"child relationship\", \"embryo\", \"transsexuals\", \"medically assisted\", \"fertilisation\", \"gps\", and \"austrian\"** featuring prominently, suggesting that cases concerning reproductive technology, legal identity, and surveillance contribute disproportionately to non-violation findings in the Law section."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"OvFsk8Ndv1qD"},"source":["Facts and Law sections independently confirm the same high-importance case clusters — the deportation/military/surveillance cluster predicting 'Violation' and the IVF/bioethics/gender recognition cluster predicting 'No Violation' appear consistently across both sections, suggesting these are robust legal patterns. The Court's wide margin of appreciation doctrine in sensitive bioethical and national security contexts is reflected directly in the vocabulary of its judgments."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"sqgYepUW2YuV"},"source":["#**7. Conclusion**"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"WpSBxyH--kEZ"},"source":["Based on our exploratory analysis, we conclude that our research question: 'Can we predict whether an Article 8 case will result in a Violation or No Violation using the linguistic features of the judgment text?' is addressable using supervised classification techniques covered in class.\n","\n","\n","Our dataset of 1,321 Article 8 cases (2010–2023), comprising 983 Violation and 282 No Violation cases after excluding procedurally terminated cases, provides a sufficient sample size for classification. The outcome variable is clearly defined and extractable from the structured conclusion column. The class imbalance (~78% Violation) will require class weighting or resampling techniques.\n","\n","The EDA has identified several promising predictive features: the text of the Facts and Law sections, show meaningful vocabulary variation across outcome classes, as demonstrated by the n-gram, scatter plot, fighting words, and word shift analyses. Case importance level and court type correlate strongly with case complexity, and respondent country, which reflects structural patterns in how different states approach Convention rights, though the dominance of Russian cases may reduce generalisability. Initial logistic regression coefficient analysis suggests that distinctive vocabulary does exist between Violation and No Violation cases, supporting the feasibility of a bag-of-words or TF-IDF approach as covered in Lab 6.\n","\n","The main limitations to acknowledge are class imbalance, structural differences between Committee and Chamber judgments which may require separate modelling, and  the data leakage risk identified in the Law section. These are manageable constraints rather than fundamental obstaclesand a classification model trained on TF-IDF features from the Facts and Law sections is expected to produce meaningful and interpretable predictions.\n","\n"]}],"metadata":{"colab":{"provenance":[{"file_id":"1_QCRqXt1-aJ9V4pmh64q2_XgBPNHrj4w","timestamp":1772892381131},{"file_id":"1M2AycZ34Au_gsaZnNJ4ynkACipED2Pc6","timestamp":1772842460834}],"toc_visible":true},"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python"}},"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":0}